# Frontend Environment Configuration

## Environment Variables

Create a `.env.local` file in the frontend folder to configure the API URL for different environments.

### Development (Default)

If using default Vite proxy to backend, no .env needed:
```bash
# .env.local (optional for development)
VITE_API_URL=/api
```

This proxies requests through `vite.config.ts` to `http://localhost:5000`.

### Production

For deployed backend:
```bash
# .env.local (for production build)
VITE_API_URL=https://api.yourdomain.com
```

### Local Backend on Different Port

If running backend on port 5001:
```bash
# .env.local
VITE_API_URL=http://localhost:5001/api
```

## Configuration Details

- `VITE_API_URL` - Base URL for all API requests
- Prefix must be `VITE_` for Vite to expose it to client
- Accessed in code as: `import.meta.env.VITE_API_URL`
- Defaults to `/api` if not set (uses Vite proxy)

## Example .env.local

```bash
# Local development
VITE_API_URL=/api

# OR remote development backend
VITE_API_URL=http://192.168.1.100:5000/api

# OR production
VITE_API_URL=https://recruiterreply-api.azurewebsites.net
```

## Building with Environment Variables

```bash
# Development build (uses Vite proxy)
npm run dev

# Production build with custom API URL
VITE_API_URL=https://api.prod.com npm run build

# Or set in .env.local before building
npm run build
```

## Troubleshooting

**"Cannot connect to backend" error:**
- Check `VITE_API_URL` in .env.local
- Ensure backend is running on the configured URL
- Check CORS settings in backend Program.cs if using non-localhost URL
- Open browser DevTools → Network tab to see actual request URL
